    <p>
      Blue boxes are model calls, yellow boxes are code. Read the lanes left to right: the chain is all blue, with the
      tables written into its prompts; the workflow is the same first blue box, then yellow; the agent is a single blue
      box in a loop, and which yellow box it reaches is up to the model. Use the ⤢ button to enlarge the diagram.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What Tells Them Apart <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      All three have gates, and all three can loop. So those aren't the difference. Two questions are:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>What are the steps?</strong> Separates the chain from the workflow.</li>
      <li><strong>Who decides the next step?</strong> Separates the workflow from the agent.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>ReAct agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Who decides the next step?</strong></td><td>Code</td><td>Code</td><td><strong>The model</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>What are the steps?</strong></td><td>All model calls</td><td>Model calls <strong>and</strong> code/functions</td><td>Whatever tool the model asks for</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where the tables live</td><td>In the prompts, as text</td><td>In code</td><td>In code, behind tools</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lookups, sums, rules done by</td><td>The model</td><td>Code, called by your code</td><td>Code, <strong>called because the model asked</strong> (tool calls)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What the loop does</td><td>Retries the same step</td><td>Retries the same step</td><td><strong>Is</strong> the design: each turn can do something different</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gates</td><td>After each step</td><td>After the model step</td><td>Final gate before the answer is accepted</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Two kinds of loop</h3>

    <p>
      The word "loop" covers two different things here. In the chain and the workflow, a loop is a <strong>retry</strong>:
      a step's output fails its gate, the error goes back to the model, and the <em>same</em> step runs again, at most
      three times. The next step is still fixed. In the agent, the loop is the <strong>engine</strong>: think, call a
      tool, observe, decide again, and each turn can go somewhere different. Both are feedback loops (generate, check,
      feed the result back); only the agent's decides where to go next.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Tool calls are what make it ReAct</h3>

    <p>
      The workflow and the agent use the same functions. In the workflow your code calls
      <code>price_damage(...)</code> at a fixed point. In the agent the model writes <code>ACT: price_damage(...)</code>,
      your code runs it, and the model reads the result before choosing what to do next. A function the model can ask
      for is a <strong>tool</strong>; the model asking is a <strong>tool call</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Step by Step, Side by Side <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>Claim C004: hail dents on the hood, roof and trunk, damaged side mirrors, a cracked window.</p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow (4.6)</th><th>ReAct agent (4.5)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Read the claim</td><td>🤖 Extract: hail, six areas (two mirrors)</td><td>🤖 The same extraction</td><td>🤖 Reads it in its first THINK</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>🤖 Finds C004 in the policy table in its prompt and applies the rule</td><td>🛠️ Code calls <code>check_coverage</code></td><td>🤖 → 🛠️ Asks for <code>check_coverage(hail)</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost</td><td>🤖 Adds up six prices from the table in its prompt</td><td>🛠️ Code sums them: $5,450</td><td>🤖 → 🛠️ Asks for <code>price_damage</code>: $5,450</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Severity</td><td>🤖 Applies the bands; the gate checks it fits the model's own total</td><td>🧮 Code: $5,450 is Major</td><td>🤖 Picks Major; the final gate checks it against the priced total</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Route</td><td>🤖 Applies the rules from its prompt</td><td>🛠️ Code calls <code>route_claim</code></td><td>🤖 → 🛠️ Asks for <code>route_claim</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>A claim that isn't covered (C005)</td><td>Still runs all four steps; the route step sends it to <code>needs_review</code></td><td>Stops after the coverage check</td><td>Usually stops after the coverage check</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who chose these steps</td><td>Your code, before any claim arrived</td><td>Your code, before any claim arrived</td><td>The model, while handling this claim</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Model calls</td><td>4, always</td><td>1</td><td>4–5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What makes the answer right</td><td>The model doing every lookup, sum and rule correctly, every time; gates catch inconsistency, not a wrong sum</td><td>Code; only the extraction can vary, and it's gated</td><td>The tools supply the facts; the final gate makes the answer match them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>With a real policy database</td><td>Breaks: millions of policies don't fit in a prompt</td><td>Works</td><td>Works</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      With the same information, all three can reach the right queue for C004 (Major, $5,450, <code>total_loss</code>).
      The difference is how. The chain asks the model to do lookups and arithmetic that code does exactly, in four calls
      for every claim, and its gates can only check that the answers are consistent with each other. The workflow does the
      same work in code with one call. The agent gets the same correctness from the same tools, but spends calls deciding
      what to do next. Run the notebook below to see real numbers, including whether the chain's sums hold up.
    </p>

