    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Trade-offs <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Prompt chain</th><th>Workflow</th><th>Agent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Who picks the next step</td><td>Code</td><td>Code</td><td>The model</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where the LLM is used</td><td>Every step</td><td>Only where text needs reading or writing</td><td>Every turn, to choose and to read</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same input, same path?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Not always</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost and latency</td><td>Medium: one call per step</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Highest, and it varies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Testing</td><td>Test each step</td><td>Unit-test the code; test the LLM steps</td><td>Test tools and gates; evaluate whole traces</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Debugging</td><td>Find the failing step</td><td>Find the failing step</td><td>Read the THINK/ACT/OBSERVE trace</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A new kind of case</td><td>New step or prompt change</td><td>New branch in code</td><td>Often a new tool or a line in a description</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Best when</td><td>Every step needs language, in a known order</td><td>Steps are known; some are rules or lookups</td><td>The path depends on what you find</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Terms <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        A <strong>prompt chain</strong> is a workflow whose steps are all LLM calls, each one's output feeding the next
        (lesson 4.2).
      </li>
      <li>
        A <strong>workflow</strong> is a fixed set of steps that your code runs, with branches. Some steps call an LLM,
        some call tools or plain functions. Lesson 4.2's "hard-coded workflow" (the dental appointment) is one.
      </li>
      <li>
        An <strong>agent</strong> is an LLM in a loop with tools, choosing its own next step until it decides it's done
        or hits a limit (lessons 2.1 and 4.5).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>They sit on a scale, from most predictable to most flexible:</p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  A["📝 Single prompt"]:::p --> B["🔗 Prompt chain"]:::p --> C["⚙️ Workflow<br/>code + LLM steps"]:::w --> D["⚙️🤖 Workflow with<br/>one agent step"]:::w --> E["🤖 Agent"]:::a
  classDef p fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef w fill:#fef3c7,color:#78350f,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:2px
  classDef a fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      Moving right buys flexibility and costs predictability, money and latency. The guide below is about moving
      only as far right as the problem needs.
    </p>

